1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,250
The Botanic Gardens and the
Rubber That Remade Malaya

2
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:08,135
Joggers, wedding photographers
and picnicking families fill

3
00:00:08,215 --> 00:00:11,832
the Singapore Botanic Gardens
most weekends, and few of

4
00:00:11,912 --> 00:00:15,864
them know they're walking through
the origin point of one of

5
00:00:15,944 --> 00:00:19,775
the twentieth century's biggest
economic transformations.

6
00:00:19,775 --> 00:00:22,763
A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2015 —

7
00:00:22,843 --> 00:00:27,254
the only tropical botanic garden
on the list — it looks like

8
00:00:27,334 --> 00:00:31,450
exactly what it is: a
beautifully kept research garden.

9
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:36,221
It doesn't look like the place that
decided, more or less single-handedly,

10
00:00:36,301 --> 00:00:39,775
that Malaya would become
the world's rubber supplier.

11
00:00:39,775 --> 00:00:42,825
The rubber itself came from a theft.

12
00:00:42,825 --> 00:00:46,864
In 1876, an Englishman
named Henry Wickham smuggled

13
00:00:46,944 --> 00:00:51,064
around 70,000 Para rubber
(Hevea brasiliensis) seeds

14
00:00:51,144 --> 00:00:55,426
out of Brazil, breaking the
country's wild monopoly on

15
00:00:55,506 --> 00:00:59,625
the world's only reliable
source of natural rubber.

16
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:03,772
Most of the roughly 2,700
seeds that germinated

17
00:01:03,852 --> 00:01:07,450
at Kew Gardens in London went to Ceylon.

18
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:11,561
A smaller batch — 22
seedlings, sent from Kew on the

19
00:01:11,641 --> 00:01:14,623
11th of June 1877 — went to Singapore,

20
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:19,700
and eleven of them were planted
at the Botanic Gardens itself.

21
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:23,700
For over a decade, that
was the end of the story.

22
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,600
Nobody wanted to grow rubber.

23
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:31,301
Malaya's planters were making their
money from coffee and gambier,

24
00:01:31,381 --> 00:01:35,800
and a slow-growing tree with no
proven market held no appeal.

25
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:41,293
When Henry Nicholas Ridley arrived
in 1888 as the Gardens' new director,

26
00:01:41,373 --> 00:01:44,389
he inherited a stand of mature, healthy,

27
00:01:44,469 --> 00:01:49,500
entirely uneconomic trees — and
became obsessed with them anyway.

28
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,002
Ridley spent the next several years
pestering anyone who would listen,

29
00:01:54,082 --> 00:01:56,817
and plenty who wouldn't
— he carried rubber

30
00:01:56,897 --> 00:02:01,596
seeds in his own pockets and pressed
them on visiting planters uninvited,

31
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:04,425
earning himself the nickname "Mad Ridley."

32
00:02:04,425 --> 00:02:09,027
His actual scientific contribution
was solving the one real problem

33
00:02:09,107 --> 00:02:13,638
holding rubber back: the wild-rubber
tapping method used in Brazil

34
00:02:13,718 --> 00:02:18,400
hacked deep into the bark and killed
the tree within a few seasons.

35
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,332
Around 1895, Ridley worked out a
shallow, diagonal herringbone cut

36
00:02:23,412 --> 00:02:28,425
that let a tree be tapped for latex
again and again without dying.

37
00:02:28,425 --> 00:02:33,225
It's essentially the same method
used on rubber trees today.

38
00:02:33,225 --> 00:02:37,825
The planters only started
listening once they had no choice.

39
00:02:37,825 --> 00:02:43,103
A coffee-leaf disease tore through
Malaya's coffee estates in the 1890s,

40
00:02:43,183 --> 00:02:47,350
wiping out the crop those
planters had been counting on.

41
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:52,643
In 1896, a Malacca-born Straits
Chinese planter named Tan Chay Yan —

42
00:02:52,723 --> 00:02:56,277
grandson of the
philanthropist Tan Tock Seng —

43
00:02:56,357 --> 00:03:02,125
used Gardens seedlings to plant a
22-acre rubber estate at Bukit Lintang.

44
00:03:02,125 --> 00:03:03,575
It worked.

45
00:03:03,575 --> 00:03:08,784
By 1898 he'd expanded into a
3,000-acre estate at Bukit Asahan;

46
00:03:08,864 --> 00:03:11,974
by 1906 that single plantation was the

47
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:16,339
largest stand of Hevea
rubber anywhere in the world,

48
00:03:16,419 --> 00:03:20,700
and other planters were
finally following his lead.

49
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,350
Then the automobile arrived.

50
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:27,379
Demand for tyres sent rubber
prices climbing through

51
00:03:27,459 --> 00:03:31,566
the 1900s into the speculative
"rubber boom" of 1910,

52
00:03:31,646 --> 00:03:36,150
and Malaya's planters had
exactly what the market wanted.

53
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:40,559
Within twenty years of Tan
Chay Yan's first planting,

54
00:03:40,639 --> 00:03:47,075
Malaya's rubber acreage went from around
345 acres to more than 2.3 million.

55
00:03:47,075 --> 00:03:50,207
By the 1920s, the country alone supplied

56
00:03:50,288 --> 00:03:53,500
roughly half the world's natural rubber.

57
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:57,525
Ridley himself never made
a cent from any of it.

58
00:03:57,525 --> 00:04:02,857
He left Malaya in 1911, spent the
rest of his long life publishing

59
00:04:02,937 --> 00:04:08,106
plant taxonomy back in England,
and died in 1956 at nearly 101 —

60
00:04:08,186 --> 00:04:12,450
outliving almost everyone
who'd once called him mad.

61
00:04:12,450 --> 00:04:15,305
Singapore's press at
the time put it plainly:

62
00:04:15,385 --> 00:04:19,625
he'd made millions for Malaya and
never made a penny in the boom.

63
00:04:19,625 --> 00:04:24,780
I've visited the Gardens myself,
most memorably to see this orchid —

64
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,628
Dendrobium Barack and Michelle Obama,

65
00:04:27,708 --> 00:04:32,632
a hybrid named for the then-US
president and First Lady in August

66
00:04:32,712 --> 00:04:37,100
2016 to mark fifty years of
US-Singapore diplomatic ties.

67
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:41,513
It's one of dozens of orchids in
the National Orchid Garden named

68
00:04:41,593 --> 00:04:46,145
after visiting heads of state over
the decades, a much gentler kind

69
00:04:46,225 --> 00:04:50,925
of horticultural diplomacy than the
one this post is actually about.

70
00:04:50,925 --> 00:04:55,590
Why it matters today: a botanic
garden most people treat as a place

71
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:59,697
for a quiet walk or a wedding
photo is where a persistent,

72
00:04:59,777 --> 00:05:04,796
unpaid civil servant turned a handful
of stolen Brazilian seeds into the

73
00:05:04,876 --> 00:05:09,550
industry that built modern Malaysia
and Singapore's early economy.

74
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:12,319
The trees themselves are largely gone now,

75
00:05:12,399 --> 00:05:16,186
but the tapping method cut
into their bark over a century

76
00:05:16,266 --> 00:05:19,725
ago is still how the world's
rubber gets harvested.
